<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eudokia Palaiologina</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1265–<a href="/years/1302">1302</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Eudokia Palaiologina, Empress of Trebizond · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palaiologos-dynasty">Palaiologos dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eudokia Palaiologina was a daughter of the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a> who married Emperor John II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> in 1282. The marriage brought Trebizond closer to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and John gave up the title of emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. She was the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-ii-of-trebizond">Alexios II of Trebizond</a>, and after John's death in 1297 she remained influential at her son's court until her death.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eudokia was the third daughter of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a> and his wife Theodora, a grandniece of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-doukas-vatatzes">John III Doukas Vatatzes</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>. Her marriage to John II Megas Komnenos, Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>, took place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1282, and the couple had two sons, Alexios and Michael. Her husband appointed her brother, Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-ii-palaiologos">Andronikos II Palaiologos</a>, as guardian of the young Alexios, which gave the court of Constantinople a say in the affairs of Trebizond after John's death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As a widow, Eudokia became a piece in her brother's diplomacy. Andronikos planned to seal a settlement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-milutin">Stefan Uroš II Milutin</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, by offering her hand in return for a negotiated peace, and the king, one of whose three wives had recently died, was willing. Eudokia refused. The historian Donald M. Nicol writes that nothing Andronikos could do would persuade her &quot;to entertain the prospect of life with a lecherous barbarian in the wilds of Serbia&quot;. Milutin had to settle instead for Simonida, Andronikos's daughter by his second wife, Irene of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a>.</p><p>A second dispute concerned her son. Alexios II chose for himself a Georgian princess, Djiadjak Jaqeli, while Andronikos, as his guardian, wanted the marriage annulled so that Alexios could marry the daughter of the high court official Nikephoros Choumnos. Eudokia won her brother's consent on the pretext of persuading her son to dissolve the marriage, and in 1301 she instead advised Alexios to keep his Georgian wife.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Eudokia died on 18 September 1302. The historian William Miller speculated that she was buried in the church of Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-nyssa">Gregory of Nyssa</a>, and Anthony Bryer noted &quot;a strong independent local nineteenth-century tradition&quot; that she founded that church before her death.</p><p>Bryer also proposed that a Eudokia who became a nun under the monastic name Euphemia, named in a painted inscription that Fallmerayer recorded at the Panagia Theoskepastos Monastery, may have been the empress. This woman has not been satisfactorily shown to belong to the Great Komnenoi, and Bryer himself raised objections to the identification. He remarked that it would not be surprising if Eudokia had wished to put a nun's veil between herself and her brother's marriage plans, which included Mongol and Turkish alliances for other women of the family.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eudokia Palaiologina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1282 John II married <b>Eudokia</b>, daughter of Michael VIII Palaiologos, and adopted a more modest title, Emperor and Autocrat of all the East, the Iberians and the Perateia, the last term referring to dependencies across the Black Sea in the Crimea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
